{"kind":"assert","text":"Part three of the kernel admission gaps — the over-ceiling attachment path — has one finding that places the work and one that blocks it. I am recording both before writing code, and this assert rests on the promise so it attaches to the commitment it qualifies.\n\nWHERE IT BELONGS, and it is not where part one was. The design says that above the genesis-declared ceiling an attachment is a content-addressed hash reference with an optional locator hint. That degrade cannot happen in the kernel, and the reason is structural rather than a matter of taste: the actor signs a canonical submission intent binding `payload` to the tree hash. If the kernel substituted a reference for oversized bytes it would change the tree, the reconstructed hash would no longer equal the signed one, and the submission would fail its own signature check. So the kernel physically cannot do this, and no contract needs to move to let it.\n\nIt belongs in internal/app, before signing. That layer already holds Config.PayloadCeiling and today performs no size check at all, so an oversized attachment is signed, submitted, and refused by the kernel with \"event exceeds genesis ceiling\" after the work of signing is already spent. Unlike part one, this needs no ruling: the boundary is already in the right place and the application side of it is simply empty.\n\nWHAT BLOCKS IT. The storage policy is unspecified, and implementing without it would produce references to bytes that git deletes. Attachment bytes are written by WritePayloadTree as blobs inside the payload tree, which is reachable from the commit, so they persist because the tree holds them. A degraded attachment puts only the reference in that tree. The bytes themselves would then be an unreferenced object, and `git gc` collects unreferenced objects. \"The hash binds, storage is a hint\" says what the reference means but not who keeps the bytes, and the honest reading is that nobody does unless something outside this repository already has them.\n\nSo a naive implementation converts a hard refusal, which is at least truthful, into a signed reference to content that will silently disappear. That is worse than the refusal it replaces, and it is the kind of failure that shows up long after the commit that caused it.\n\nI am not choosing the policy alone. My recommendation is the one that stores nothing implicitly: require the locator hint when an attachment degrades, so the caller states where the bytes live and the log never implies custody it does not have. The alternative is for the repository to keep degraded bytes under a ref of its own so gc cannot reach them, which makes the hash bind something retrievable but quietly grows storage the ceiling exists to bound.\n\nParts one and two are unaffected. Part two is at head f116097a awaiting codex's review; part one remains blocked on hugh's ruling in bf399575."}